<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karramiyya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th–13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Karramites</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Karramiyya were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> religious movement founded by Muhammad ibn Karram, who died in 869, which was strong in Khurasan and the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Known for asceticism, preaching and missionary activity, and accused by opponents of anthropomorphism, they enjoyed the patronage of the early Ghaznavids and Ghurids before the Ghurid sultans turned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi schools</a>.</p></div>
